  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential relationship between Npc191948 and LS174T through a series of target interactions involving Leosibirone B. This is supported by multiple paths, with Leosibirone B acting as a common mediator in all three connections. In Path 1, Leosibirone B targets Ataxin-2, which is directly linked to Npc191948. In Path 2, Leosibirone B targets the 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14, which is also linked to Npc191948. In Path 3, Leosibirone B targets Ataxin-2 again, but also interacts with Seneciphylline, which in turn targets LS174T. These findings collectively indicate that Npc191948 may influence the activity of Leosibirone B, which then modulates the function of Ataxin-2 and potentially other proteins, leading to a downstream effect on LS174T. The mechanism appears to involve Leosibirone B as a central node in a complex network of interactions, with Npc191948 playing a role in the regulation of its targets. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between Npc191948 and LS174T through the indirect pathway involving Leosibirone B, though further validation would be needed to confirm the direct relationship.",
